# Title

One paragraph: what breaks, or what you are trying to do, and why the obvious approach
does not work. Lead with the consequence, not the mechanism — a reader who recognises
their symptom here will keep reading.

## The thing itself

State behaviour, then consequence:

> A malformed include is dropped silently — Kodi logs a parse error and renders the
> window without it, so a missing element is a parse failure until proven otherwise.

Give commands, not descriptions of commands:

```sh
kodi-remote get Application.GetProperties version
```

Include measurements if you have them. "0.62 s → 0.17 s" is checkable; "faster" is not.

Name versions wherever behaviour diverges. "Omega refuses, Piers sniffs" is the useful
form; "depends on your version" is not.

## What fails silently

The highest-value section in most skills. An agent can discover a loud failure by
running into it. A silent one it will never find, and will build on top of.

## Verifying it

How a reader confirms this themselves in under a minute. If there is a one-liner that
proves the behaviour, put it here — it makes the skill self-checking as Kodi changes.

## Open questions

Anything you could not confirm. Content under this heading is exempt from the hedge
lint, so this is where uncertainty belongs — say plainly what you do not know and what
would settle it.

Delete this section if there is nothing in it.

